ei":12090,"sm":{"ts_it":false,"ts_it_i":true}}],"revision":17145}; DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart = new Date().getTime(); DOCS_initialLoadTiming.incrementTime('mp', DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart - DOCS_modelChunkParseStart); DOCS_warmStartDocumentLoader.loadModelChunk(DOCS_modelChunk); DOCS_modelChunk = undefined;</script><script nonce="lCe9daBLmXLzAL2VaOmfEw">DOCS_modelChunkParseStart = new Date().getTime();</script><script nonce="lCe9daBLmXLzAL2VaOmfEw">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":12091,"s":"Going forward:\nUse DOIs whenever possible for source \u0026 referenceID.\nReference validation process will resolve DOIs and add citations to the References master file.\nIf no globally unique identifier is available:\ncreate a WikiData record for the reference, which will be resolved by reference validation process OR \ncreate an identifier (i.e., hash) and add the reference \u0026 citation to the references-without-identifier list.\nIf there is another globally unique identifier like a handle available, use that identifier. If it doesn’t resolve to a citation, add the citation once it shows up in the references-without-citation list.\nData Products\ntaxonID [eolID \u003d pageID]\u000bscientificNameID [link to the external reference hierarchy]\u000bhigherClassification [according to the EOL hierarchy]\u000bscientificName [according to the trait data source]\u000bscientificName [according to the EOL hierarchy]\u000bmeasurementType\u000bmeasurementTypeURI\u000bmeasurementValue\u000bmeasurementValueURI\u000bmeasurementUnit\u000bmeasurementUnitURI\u000bmeasurementRemarks\u000bmeasurementMethod\u000bstatisticalMethod\u000btargetTaxon\u000btargetTaxonSourceName\u000btargetTaxonName\u000bsex\u000blifeStage\u000bsource\t\u000bbibliographicCitation\u000breferenceID\u000bresourceURL\f\nNotes on Taxonomic Requirements\nAccepted Names in the Graph\nWe currently have two kinds of names in the graph: the preferred canonicalName for the mapped eolID and the scientificName represented in the trait data file. I think it may be a good idea to use the EOL scientificName rather than the canonical in the graph. This would make it easier to distinguish homonyms, but it may make querying more complicated because people usually search using canonical strings, not full scientificNames. Maybe we could have both in the graph, if that doesn’t create too much overhead? Consult with Jorrit.\nSynonyms in the Graph\nDo we want to have synonyms in the graph? They could be useful if we want to let people search on them.\nIf we want synonyms, how should we treat them in the data model? Treating them as properties would be simpler, but then we couldn\u0027t have their scientificNameIDs and other metadata in the graph. Is it enough if this information is available in the EOL hierarchy file?\nCurrent thoughts: We should treat synonyms as nodes in the graph, and we should allow traits to be attached to synonyms in the Traits file. In the graph, the trait data would then be attached to the acceptedName via its synonym. This would automatically transfer the trait mapping to the new acceptedName when the hierarchy is updated. We currently have many traits where the source uses a synonym, and we have manually mapped it to the current accepted name. We could gradually clean those up, but there will probably always be some source names that we will not want to map as synonyms, e.g., gross misspellings. \nHigherClassification Data in the Graph\nShould we store higherClassification ID strings in addition to parent taxa in the graph? This would make the graph a lot larger, but it may make queries more efficient. Consult with Jorrit. \nLegacy Taxa\nThere will be taxa in our trait data sets that cannot be cleanly mapped to any accepted taxa, because there are no synonym mappings available and/or mapping would require a deep understanding of the taxon concept used in the trait data source. \nFor species it’s probably ok to tentatively place the name in the EOL hierarchy, even if we know it’s not in current use. This preserves the trait data record, and we can always change the mapping later if we can figure it out. \nBut this is a real problem for inferred traits from older sources, e.g., the genera from the Kubitzki data set. How should we handle genera and other higher taxa if we know the trait data set name is not in use anymore or its concept has changed significantly? I don’t think we should account for this situation in the data model, but we should talk about best practices. For example, we may decide to include data for such taxa as individual data points, but not use them for taxonomic inference. Data like this may be useful for data users who have the knowledge to do their own mappings of taxon concepts.\n"},{"ty":"ae","et":"list","id":"kix.cgu2u9yk0aqo","epm":{"le_nb":{"nl_4":{"b_gf":"%4","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":false,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_bgc2_i":true,"ts_it":false,"ts_va":"nor","ts_bd_i":true,"ts_va_i":true,"ts_fs":11.0,"ts_ff":"Arial","ts_st":false,"ts_bd":false,"ts_fgc2_i":true,"ts_sc_i":true,"ts_tw":400},"b_ifl":162.0,"b_gs":"○","b_a":0,"b_sn":1,"b_gt":9,"b_il":180.0},"nl_3":{"b_gf":"%3","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":false,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_bgc2_i":true,"ts_it":false,"ts_va":"nor","ts_bd_i":true,"ts_va_i":true,"ts_fs":11.0,"ts_ff":"Arial","ts_st":false,"ts_bd":false,"ts_fgc2_i":true,"ts_sc_i":true,"ts_tw":400},"b_ifl":126.0,"b_gs":"●","b_a":0,"b_sn":1,"b_gt":9,"b_il":144.0},"nl_2":{"b_gf":"%2","b_ts":{"ts_un":false,"ts_un_i":false,"ts_sc":false,"ts_it_i":true,"ts_fgc2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0},"ts_st_i":true,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":null,"clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":true,"ts_bgc2_i":true,"ts_it":false