9daBLmXLzAL2VaOmfEw">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":16183,"s":"Duplicate Taxa in Catalogue of Life\nThere’s a lot of taxon duplication in Catalogue of Life. There are things like unrecognized synonyms, alternative representations from WoRMS, whole branches imported twice from different suppliers with different names at the root but overlapping genera and species. This often happens in branches with fossil taxa. I am inclined to omit these taxa from the EOL hierarchy, but if I do this, I will have to pick a winner, and I need to develop a procedure to mark the losers, so we know they have been excluded as duplicates in future updates. There are also complications with merging the descendants of partially duplicated branches. If I move some taxa from one COL ancestor to another, I am changing the reference hierarchy, which takes us back into more serious hierarchy management territory. I was trying to avoid this, but it may be necessary.\nTaxonomic Data from Trait Data Sources\nTaxonomic data provided by the trait data source could go into a separate Taxon file. I don\u0027t think these data should go into the graph, but they could be harvested from the resource file for extended data products.\nNote that there may be scenarios where the taxonomic data provided by the trait data source conflicts with the EOL hierarchy. This will not be apparent in the resource file and may lead to confusion.\nTaxon Documentation in Data Products\nFor names, we want both the EOL scientificName and the scientificName from the trait data source. Higher classification (according to our management hierarchy) could be an optional field. What do we want to provide in terms of identifiers? In addition to the TraitBank taxonID (\u003deolID), we may want to provide the scientificNameID which is the link to the external hierarchy resource (usually COL). \nWhat if the trait data file has GUIDs as taxonIDs or provides their own mapping to external GUIDs outside EOL. Shouldn’t we include those in data products? If so, we either need to add them to the trait properties.\nTransparency for Data Users\nWe should include an eml file or something similar in both the resource files and the data packages that explain our taxonomic mapping rationale along with other things like the ontology mappings.\nTaxonomic Data in Input Files\nInitial scenario, now dismissed:\nTrait files will have a taxonID (\u003deolID) column that links to taxonID values in the management hierarchy whenever possible. \ntaxonID values that are simple integers link to the management hierarchy\ntaxonID values that are not simple integers represent unmapped nodes\nThese are in the Trait file in order to represent all trait information from the source, but they will not go into the graph\nUnmapped nodes may become mappable with additional research\nThe taxonID values of unmapped nodes can be used to link to additional taxonomic information in the Taxon file.\nTrait files will have a scientificName column. This preserves the name attached to the trait record in the source work.\nThis may be the canonical name or the full scientific name with author data, whatever is easier to grab/mine from the resource.\nTaxon file: \nThe taxonID links to the management hierarchy\nMapping nodes from the management hierarchy can be included to support complex taxon mappings for synonyms and other names not represented in the management hierarchy.\nWhat if the source work provides GUIDs as taxonIDs or provides their own mapping to external GUIDs outside EOL? This will be rare.\nWe could represent this information with additional identifier columns. \nShould we have this information in the graph, i.e., included in standard data products?\nNotes on Other Stuff\nGeographic Data\nGoing forward, only harvest geographic data that is expert curated, eg., from curated databases, published country checklists, etc. \nMake sure charismatic groups like mammals \u0026 birds are well covered. \nThen do a gap analysis